A||||22||KnightRyder31||1970 Dodge Challenger ||||1||
Z||000000||KnightRyder31||03-12-2001||10:35 PM||||Hey guys, anyone know anything about this kind of car?<BR>It's my dream car, and I would like to know everything about this car.<BR>Any info would be great, like performence stats, options, ect.<BR>I will own one some day, when I have the money.<BR>I think they run about 20K fully restored(with the 383 or 440). I will never make enough money to buy a HEMI Challenger, I think they go for 75K and up.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||03-12-2001||10:48 PM||||I had a 72 340ci automatic, it would smoke the tires like a mesquito sprayer. I had the 436 hemi in another car built on same platform, Plymouth Valient. There great cars but suffered from Mopars lack of quality control at the time, so there pretty affordable. My only real complaint was I was always twisting the driveshaft or rear end pinion out of it. Personally I liked the 340 and there about 1/2 the price of the rest. if you want a big block, but cant affort the hemi, I think the 383 Magnum is the best. The only ones that are way up pricy are the special editions like the ARA, AAR. Cuda is the same car as Challenger too. Just a little different exterior trim. A 340 SixPack (3-2bbl carbs) was a monster.<P>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-12-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||KnightRyder31||03-12-2001||11:13 PM||||Do you know the top end on that baby?<BR>Or any times for that manor?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||wahoo4u||03-13-2001||01:33 AM||||I have friends with a 70 challenger with a 318.  Not a big v8 by any means, yet the car screams.  Top end is somewhere around 125 on this car.  There dad bought it brand new in 70 and has had it ever since.  130,000 miles and all original.  Needs some restoring but still a hell of a car.  If you are looking for one I would suggest starting in Arizona or down that way.  You can usually find one in a salvage yard that will need restoring.  If you are looking for one already restored, be prepared to spend some bucks.  They are nice cars, that is for sure.  I have had great times in that car.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Raydar||03-13-2001||09:32 AM||||I've seen restored (actually customized) and original Barracudas and Challengers in GA for 5K to 9K. If you find one that's running it's probably either been babied or completely gone through. Roger is right about the quality control. I had a 73 Barracuda that felt like it was made out of recycled Coors cans. Rode like a buckboard wagon. Also had a 71 that started to rust, and had electrical problems and a driveline vibration. <BR>If I wasn't hip-deep in Fiero projects, I would probably try to find another Barracuda. (Shhhhh. Don't tell my wife. She wants a 72 Chevelle.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||03-13-2001||11:35 AM||||ya Raydar, I was always a die hard mopar fan till they went to front wheel drive platform<P>1960 Fury convertible 318 w/police interceptor dual 4bbl<P>1961 Fury convertible 318<P>1969 Super Bee 426 cross ram 2-4bbl<P>1963 Valient convertible (swap) 426 hemi ran over 150 but wayyyyyyy to scary<P>1972 Challenger 340 4bbl<P>1966 Barracuda RS 273 V8<P>1969 Barracuda fastback 426 Hemi 4 bbl<P>1954? Chrysler 300 347? Hemi<P>1957 Chrylser Imperial 392 Hemi<P>1970 Dodge Dart Swinger 340 SixPack<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||KnightRyder31||03-13-2001||12:49 PM||||Hey Roger, you had a HEMI????<BR>Damn! How do they run?<BR>I bet they're fast as hell!||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||03-13-2001||01:00 PM||||ya, i used to LOVE Mopars as you can see. Had a lot with hemis all sizes. Pro drag racers still use them prob 80% of the time. Most reliable and strongest one was the 392. They have enormous torque. The valves in the hemisphereical head are huge. Ferrari uses the same type of head design with 5 valves per cylinder.||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||03-13-2001||06:27 PM||||The fuel cars for the most part have engines that are modeled after the early Hemi,but like Nascar cars have nothing in common with the original except the name.Those Hemi engines were probably the heaviest engine ever built for a passenger car.<BR>Roger,the 54 was a 354ci engine.The earlier engines were 331ci.||||reg||1
Z||000009||87GT||03-13-2001||06:32 PM||||My cousin has a 70 Challenger with a 440.  He is trying to get rid of it, but its in pretty rough shape.  He has the engine out of it and in his shop.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||SpeedPhreak||03-13-2001||07:34 PM||||i had a 74 charger.. i loved that car.. i was actually looking for a cuda/challenger when i came across it.  it only had a 225 but the body was very straight.  i wanted to drop in a 440 soooo bad.  i miss that car alot.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cheever2||03-13-2001||07:55 PM||||I remember test driving a used 70 Challenger 340.  My dad wouldn't co-sign for it.  That must've been 1976, but I cherish the brief memory of that car to this day.<BR>Once had a 71 Satellite Sebring w/318 and loved it, but no comparison I guess.<BR>||||reg||6
