Z||000020||Patrick's Dad||03-22-2001||08:11 PM||||Also try <A HREF="http://www.valiant.org." TARGET=_blank>www.valiant.org.</A> <P>I own a '74 Valaint with the 318. Trying to figure out whether to rebuild (Only 89K) or replace with a 360. The tranny's a pain; only a 3 speed, and, man, does the thing rev at highway speeds. I'll probably run larger wheels and/or swap out the rear end gears to improve highway driving.<P>Right now, it'll smoke the tires out of the gate, but not much else. The chassis is loose, even compared to my wife's Explorer (Or, POS, in my nomenclature), but it's competent enough. I'm also planning on chopping 6" off the windshield and maybe shaving the doorhandles. I haven't given it too much thought, because it's an effective daily driver while I work the Fiero....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/StarHeroSig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||rogergarrison||03-23-2001||05:43 AM||||Man, you must have a very low rear gear. I cut and stuffed a 426 hemi in my Valiant. It only turned like 1500 RPM at 75 mph. If I remember the 360 is basicly same block as the 318 so that ought to be almost a bolt in. The 360 from the 'Lil Red Truck' was a sweet runner. Id keep the trans. The 727 was about the best, most bulletproof ever made next to Mercedes.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Raydar||03-23-2001||02:17 PM||||FWIW, I seem to remember that the left-side motormount is different between a 318 and a 360. <BR>If you're talking about the 360 Magnum crate motor, I understand that it was a major redesign. Not sure what, if anything, is compatible. Also, the stock tranny in the 318 Valiant is probably a 904. Lighter duty than a 727. My 73 318 Barracuda had the 904. Unlike a 727, it was <I>not</I> unbreakable.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
